 to lots of different kinds of things but  it's generic enough that that's not a  problem  if if one could call them sort of  quantum branching states or something  that one's kind of stuck talking about  them you know once they're  very confused oh it's quantum branching  states but i'm using it to describe  biological evolution or something like  that it's it's rather confusing  so i i found it useful to go back to uh  sort of word roots and there are some  books probably even have them behind me  but which are  i have books of word roots and things  like this and i find those at least  useful for some inspiration  um about possible ways to name things  you know there's some things that i've  been trying to name for  years and i just haven't got a good name  for them  i mean one thing that i tried to name  for years probably 20 years  was some uh wolfram language  um in fact i wrote a whole uh post about  uh the effort to name wolfram language  we eventually named it  in a way that's sort of meaningless  because it just has  you know a name on it that isn't a  meaningful name it's not a name that  says  it's a language about representing  things computationally it's just  says it's just sort of a random word  that happens to be  you know my last name but that's that's  just in a sense a random word  um it's um uh so for example  will from alpha another thing which took  a long time to name  and uh again we didn't want to give it a  name that was too specific  you know like you know answer system you  know  or or like you know uh ansabot or i  don't know what it might have been  called but but  um uh well we have long lists of  possible names  but so one thing i've been trying to  name uh and i'd love to get any help i  can get  is the name for the abstract science of  studying the computational universe  and that's what my big book a new kind  of science um  is about and so that field often gets  sort of called  nks uh kind of as a after the acronym  for the book  but i'd really like to find a name that  represents  kind of that process of sort of  exploring  life in the computational universe you  know i i thought about you know cyber  zoology or something but that's not the  right name  it's a weird name um but anyway so i've  been i've been trying to um  uh i've been trying to get um uh trying  to figure that out  for a long time um  lots of interesting questions here one  from alan about some  clouds and water vapor i'd love to  answer that question um  but let's not do that this time i think  i have to come back and do that next  time  um the question here from swsh  about learning new fields i thought  since i've been talking a little bit  about that i'll try and address that  here  question is whether it's better to start  from modern digested materials  or to go back to the original papers in  a field or original documents in a field  when it was just being formulated  that's a very interesting question so  i think it's worth always trying the  original documents  sometimes they had a  notation that's very hard to understand  things have really been streamlined  since but sometimes they said it much  more clearly  than the people who came later and  sometimes what's happened is  that they said something i don't know  um girdle for example said something in  his paper  and at the time it was pretty hard to  understand because it was very fresh and  new  but then subsequently people  built all kinds of stuff on top of that  and so the things that were in there  become much more familiar and then you  can go back and look at the original you  already have some ambient knowledge of  some of the concepts that are in there  and the original is easier to understand  than it was for people back when it was  first produced  so i'm always a believer in trying to go  back to the source materials and often  they're sometimes quite hard to find and  sometimes they've been abridged  shortened  because people thought there was a lo