cies  actually his first edition was better  than subsequent editions in many ways  because  in the first edition he just said here's  what i think is true  but by the third edition of something i  think he got to third edition  um he was like professor so-and-so has  made the following objection  and i'm going to answer that here well  actually nobody cares anymore about  professor so-and-so's objection it  turned out to be kind of silly  um but it really damages the flow of  of what darwin was trying to say so  sometimes you'll get these things which  are of their time  you know where there was some ambient  understanding um  and uh that under ambient understanding  had to be sort of cast aside i mean when  i wrote my new kind of science book  um i was very aware of these issues and  so  i tried to avoid i mean i just kind of  said what was true some people didn't  necessarily like that but i just sort of  said what was true  and and did only in the notes at the  back did i kind of say how does it  relate to  things that people have said before and  you know what do you have to sort of  push aside to understand this  um the uh  um that's a funny thing here uh  quoted by push here um from my  late friend marvin minsky apparently he  said about ai research as marvin said  his problem was that if you look at  papers published between 1950 and 1970  they seem to be 10 years ahead of the  ones that you read now there's been lots  of research and it's going backwards  said marvin  well ai was a funny field and marvin had  a funny place in that funny field  um so i uh ai was a field  that went took some took some funny  turns  um but uh um uh  anyway there's some suggestions from  mark here about auto  uh various possible names for for nks  interesting they don't quite make it but  but  interesting i i have played with the  word auto  um or the root auto at great length all  right unfortunately i have to sign off  here because i am uh  uh late for other things but it's a